# =============================================================================
#  Reversible first-order conversion      A <--kf/kr--> B
# =============================================================================
#  A single reversible reaction: A converts to B at rate kf, B back to A at rate
#  kr. The total A + B is conserved, and the system relaxes to an equilibrium set
#  by the ratio of the rates:
#
#        dA/dt = -kf*A + kr*B          A(t) -> A_eq = kr/(kf+kr) * (A0+B0)
#        dB/dt =  kf*A - kr*B          B(t) -> B_eq = kf/(kf+kr) * (A0+B0)
#
#  Two very different measurements probe this system, and NEITHER pins both rates
#  on its own -- which is the point of lesson 30:
#
#    * EQUILIBRIUM (a steady-state titration): vary the total amount and read the
#      equilibrium B. This sees only the RATIO kf/kr (the equilibrium constant);
#      it cannot tell a fast pair (kf,kr) from a slow pair with the same ratio.
#    * KINETICS (a relaxation time-course): watch B approach equilibrium. The
#      relaxation rate is kf+kr -- the timescale the equilibrium data is blind to.
#
#  Fit them TOGETHER (plus a qualitative fact) and the ratio + the rate pin both
#  kf and kr. Adapted from the analytical_odes `reversible_first_order_conversion`
#  model (closed-form report functions, Clock/t, and the A_eq/B_eq/T_total/s_rate
#  scaffolding parameters removed; the reaction network is unchanged).
# =============================================================================

begin model

  begin parameters
    kf  0.7    # /day  forward  A -> B     <- FITTED
    kr  0.2    # /day  reverse  B -> A     <- FITTED
    A0  80.0   # AU    initial A  (the total swept in the titration; B starts at 0)
    B0  0.0    # AU    initial B
  end parameters

  begin molecule types
    A()
    B()
  end molecule types

  begin seed species
    A()  A0
    B()  B0
  end seed species

  begin observables
    Molecules  Obs_A  A()
    Molecules  Obs_B  B()
  end observables

  begin reaction rules
    R_forward: A() -> B()  kf
    R_reverse: B() -> A()  kr
  end reaction rules

end model
